# RouterNodeOperationOriginSites.allow — the src-side router-as-lifecycle-origin ratchet (#1140, #4463).
#
# Each line is a PRODUCTION file that still issues a node- or hub-LIFECYCLE message without hopping
# onto MeshExtensions.NodeOperationIssuingHub():
#
#     hub.Observe(new CreateNodeRequest(node), …)      hub.Post(new MoveNodeRequest(…), …)
#     hub.Post(new DisposeRequest { … }, o => o.WithTarget(new Address(path)))
#
# For a caller holding the DI-injected IMessageHub — which in the mesh's ROOT container IS the
# ROUTER — that message leaves stamped `Sender = mesh/{id}` and its response is addressed straight
# back at `mesh/{id}`; if it is target-less it also EXECUTES on the router's single-threaded action
# block, starving the routing it exists to do (prod 2026-06-11). NodeOperationIssuingHub is the
# IDENTITY FUNCTION for every hub that is not the router, so adopting it is a no-op everywhere
# except where it matters.
#
# 🚨 THE DENOMINATOR IS DERIVED, NOT LISTED. The guard reads the lifecycle families out of
# production — MessageHub's constructor registrations (DisposeRequest, ShutdownRequest, PingRequest,
# InitializeHubRequest) and MeshExtensions.WithNodeOperationHandlers (the node-CRUD verbs) — and
# filters them through RouterTrafficRule itself. #4463 is why: the first version of the guard
# carried a literal list of six CRUD names, DisposeRequest was not on it, and MeshOperations.Recycle
# posted one straight off the router in production without reddening anything. Do not re-introduce a
# list; if a lifecycle message is missing from the denominator, the registration it should be in is
# the thing to look at.
#
# THE ENTRY BELOW IS NOT DEBT. It is the #981 self-targeted inner create inside the
# CreateOrUpdateNodeRequest HANDLER: posted on, and handled by, the hub whose turn loop already owns
# the upsert, with `o.WithTarget(hub.Address)` making that explicit. Hopping it would move the inner
# create off the hub that is mid-upsert, and a self-post is deliberately reported by NEITHER
# ROUTER_TRAFFIC site (see Doc/Architecture/RouterTrafficDetection, "What neither site sees").
# It is NOT covered by the guard's self-directed exclusion, and deliberately so: that exclusion is
# for HUB lifecycle only, because a target-less node CRUD post on the router is the prod 2026-06-11
# wedge and the seam does fix it. Do not "fix" this line; do not add a second line to make a new
# site pass.
#
# Format: relative/path.cs<TAB>count   ('#' starts a comment)

src/MeshWeaver.Mesh.Contract/MeshExtensions.cs	1
